.TH HEALTH 1 "2026-10-27" "health 0.2.1" "health"
.SH NAME
health \- the machine's health as a harmonograph, live in the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B health
.RI [ fps ]
.RB [ \-\-braille ]
.br
.B health \-\-once
.RB [ \-\-no\-legend ]
.RB [ \-\-cols
.IR N ]
.br
.B health \-\-png
.I path
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR WxH ]
.RB [ \-\-readings
.IR spec ]
.br
.B health \-\-demo
.I dir
.RB [ \-\-fps
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR WxH ]
.RB [ \-\-seconds
.IR N ]
.br
.B health \-h
|
.B \-\-help
|
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B health
draws the trace of a damped two-pendulum harmonograph, as large as the window, with one
muted line of readings under it, on its own screen. It turns continuously and is redrawn
from fresh readings about
.I fps
times a second (default 10), so it can be watched answering the machine: start a build and
the trace spirals in and warms in colour; let it idle and the figure locks again. Press
.B q
to leave; the previous screen comes back untouched.
.PP
The figure is a Lissajous curve. Two pendulums, one for X and one for Y, locked at a simple
ratio, draw one closed curve that stands still: the idle machine. Every reading is a
departure from that picture, zero at idle: the cpu temperature detunes the ratio (the
figure precesses and fills in) and sets the hue, blue at 35 C to red at 80 C; the load per
core damps it (each lap smaller); the memory in use adds a wobble; the disk used spreads
the colour along the trace, one hue when empty, the whole wheel when full. The hour of day
picks the ratio and the uptime turns the figure slowly; those two are the clock, not
health.
.PP
The readings reach the figure with inertia: temperature and memory over about three
seconds, load over two, and the hour's ratio over about a minute, so the figure never
shivers with the sensor and glides from one family to the next. The line under the figure
shows the raw readings.
.PP
On a terminal that draws pixels the frame is a PNG sent over the Kitty graphics protocol
(Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm, also through tmux with
.B allow-passthrough on
), the iTerm2 inline-image protocol, or as sixels through
.BR chafa (1)
when it is installed. Anywhere else, or with
.BR \-\-braille ,
the trace is Braille dots in 24-bit colour.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I fps
Frames a second, 0.5 to 20. A Raspberry Pi 5 renders a frame in about 30 ms; over a slow
link a lower rate is smoother.
.TP
.B \-\-braille
Braille dots instead of pixels, whatever the terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-once
One drawing from the readings now with the readings: user and host, uptime, ip, cpu
temperature, load, memory, disk, kernel. Instant, no animation; the form for a login
screen. On a terminal that draws pixels it is one frame of the live view, 16 rows tall,
with the readings below it; anywhere else, or with
.BR \-\-braille ,
a Braille drawing with the readings beside it.
.B \-\-no\-legend
leaves out the readings,
.BI \-\-cols " N"
sets the width instead of asking the terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-png " path"
One frame to a PNG (default 1200x600, set with
.BR \-\-size ),
from the readings now or as pinned with
.BR \-\-readings ,
a comma-separated list such as
.BR temp=73,load=4.7,mem=0.6,disk=0.8,hour=14 :
temperature in C, load average, memory and disk as fractions used, the hour.
.TP
.BI \-\-demo " dir"
Frames of a scripted scenario (a build starts, runs, and the machine cools; eighteen
seconds) through the live view's own frame maker, as
.I frame-NNNN.png
with the readings in
.IR frame-NNNN.txt ,
for a GIF of the renderer.
.BR \-\-fps ,
.B \-\-size
and
.B \-\-seconds
set the rate, the frame and the length.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HEALTH_FORMAT
.BR kitty ", " iterm " or " sixels
forces the pixel protocol; anything else forces Braille.
.TP
.B HEALTH_DEBUG
Set to log the terminal's replies and each frame's size on stderr.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the one temporary PNG per frame is written (default
.IR /tmp ),
removed on exit.
.SH FILES
None. Nothing is written outside
.BR TMPDIR .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chafa (1),
.BR htop (1),
.BR uptime (1).
.PP
https://github.com/arian-shamaei/health
